  "scale_info": {
    "name": "Sickle Cell Disease Health-Related Internal Stigma - Adolescent and Adult",
    "code": "PX870301",
    "abbreviation": "PX870301",
    "description": "The Measure of Sickle Cell Stigma (MoSCS) is an 11-item, self-administered questionnaire that measures the amount of total stigma experienced by sickle cell disease patients. The protocol includes 4 subscales measuring social exclusion, internalized stigma, disclosure concerns, and expected discrimi",
    "domain": "Sickle Cell Disease - Stigma",
    "citation": "Bulgin, D., Asnani, M., Vorderstrasse, A., Royal, C., Pan, W., & Tanabe, P. (2021). Stigma and quality of life in adults with sickle cell disease in Jamaica and the United States. Psychology, Health & Medicine, 1-15. doi:10.1080/13548506.2021.2019808 Holloway, B. M., McGill, L. S., & Bediako, S. M. (2017). Depressive symptoms and sickle cell pain: The moderating role of internalized stigma. Stigma and Health, 2(4), 271-280. doi:10.1037/sah0000060 [PhenX: https://www.phenxtoolkit.org/protocols/view/870301]",
    "license": "PhenX Toolkit freely available protocol",
